  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale\nWe address the problem of finding a step function h: [0,2] -> [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum cross-correlation integral C_5. The key insight is that the objective involves a max function, which is convex, and the constraints are linear. We formulate the problem as a linear program (LP) by relaxing the continuous integral into a sum over discrete samples. We then use a branch-and-bound algorithm with LP-based node selection and incorporate problem-specific reductions (e.g., symmetry-breaking to prune the search tree) to efficiently find an improved upper bound on the Erds minimum overlap problem constant C.\n\nThe central idea is to represent the step function as a binary vector, and use branch-and-bound with LP-based bounds at each node. The algorithm starts from a feasible solution (a flat step function) and systematically explores the space of binary representations of h, recording the best lower bound on C found during the search.\n\nThis approach directly leverages the convexity of the objective and constraints, ensuring rigorously proven improvements to the upper bound of the Erds constant.\n\nMathematical Specification\nWe represent the step function h as a binary vector s \u2208 {0,1}^{n_points} with sum(s) = n_points/2, corresponding to 1 at the left endpoint of each interval [x_i, x_{i+1}) and 0 elsewhere. The continuous integral \n    C_5 = max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\nis discretized as \n    c = (1/n_points) * max_k \u2211_{i} s[i] * (1 - s[i+k])\nfor shifts k (grid-aligned and via mode='full' convolution). The optimization problem is:\n\nMinimize c\nSubject to:\n  s[i] \u2208 {0,1} \u2200i\n  \u2211_{i} s[i] = n_points/2\n  c \u2265 (1/n_points) * \u2211_{i} s[i] * (1 - s[i+k]) for all k\n\nWhere k indexes over all grid-aligned shifts accounted for by mode='full' convolution.\n\nThe branch-and-bound scheme:\n  - At root node: begin with a LP relaxation using fractional solutions.\n  - At integer nodes: use heuristics to find integer solutions.\n  - Branch by fixing variables to 0 or 1 at fractional nodes.\n  - Use LP bounds with Gurobi's built-in presolvers.\n\nImplementation Procedure\n0. User inputs: n_points, parent_h (if available), seed\n1. Initialize: \n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - Convert parent_h (if available) to solution candidate\n   - Formulate base LP relaxation (variables, constraints)\n   - Compute initial bound using mode='full' correlation if parent available, else sample\n2. Branch-and-bound tree traversal:\n   - Maintain global best solution and value\n   - Use depth-first search with variable selection based on reduced costs\n   - Post minimal valid shifts for integer programming branch\n   - Reduce symmetry via symmetry-breaking constraints\n   - Use commercial solver (Gurobi) with slicing planes\n3. Node handling:\n   - At node, solve LP relaxation to get upper bound on c\n   - If fractional, branch by fixing variable with maximum fractional part\n   - If feasible, update global minimum integer solution\n   - Add clique constraints to tighten bound\n   - Use cutting planes for faster convergence\n4. Termination: best found solution meets n_points/2 equality and 0-1 bounds\n5. Output: best solution as step function and C_5 value\n\nSearch choices include:\n  - Starting from parent solution (n_points=70) if available\n  - Bracketing for tight bounds between 0 and 1\n  - Objective scaling for faster identification of upper bounds\n  - Depth-first search with limited depth per branch\n\nComputational budget: Estimate 100 seconds for n_points=70-200 boundaries. Optimization of shifts: may cut search time by 2X.\n\nValidation and return contract:\n  - Steps to validate: n_points must be integer, sum(s)=n_points/2 exactly, 0<=s[i]<=1\n  - Re-evaluate final candidate with mode='full' convolution via NumPy\n  - Return (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) in strict format\n\nWe change resolution gradually and validate with parent fallback.",
